From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 00:37:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fit a fixed layout's percentage columns in what its length
 columns leave
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The fixed table layout sized every percentage column from the table's
width alone, whatever its length columns took: a 600px table with 59px
of length columns and 93% of percentage columns laid its row out 617px
wide, and the last cells hung out of the table's side. Browsers give
the length columns their width first and the percentage columns the
smaller of what they ask for and what is left, in proportion to each
one's share, so the row ends where the table does.

A percentage column is now a track the table resolves itself at layout
time, when the table's width is known, instead of a stylo calc() value
that knows only its own percentage. Percentages that fit resolve as they
did.

beep-boop-🤖
